(A) Theorem 4.5(a),(b): closed forms against enumeration of the whole class (exact rationals; u in {1,2,3,5}; w(f) in {1/2, 1/3, 2^-10};
    data with repeated inputs), 198 cases: all equal: True
    u = 2^20, K = 1000: beta = 22.000001, (K-2)/beta = 45.36;  V at k = 0, 44, 45, 46, 47: 1, 0.999958, 0.998981, 0.000244065, 5.82037e-11
    Theorem 4.5(d): min of V over k <= min(u/4, (K-2)/beta) = 0.998981 (claimed > 0.51);  (e) at k = 47: V = 5.82e-11 <= 2^(K - k beta) = 5.82e-11
(B) Corollary 4.6 (memorisers closed under conditioning with C_R = C + 1), exact:
    u = 3, K = 12, C_R = 2, one pair seen, unseen input: Pr[violating answer] = 0.427111 (formula 0.427111, floor 1 - 1/C_R = 0.5000);
        Pr[no answer] = 0.427111;  Pr[an answer that abides] = 0.145778;  violation probability GIVEN that an answer is produced = 0.745540
    u = 4, K = 30, C_R = 5, one pair seen, unseen input: Pr[violating answer] = 0.711111 (formula 0.711111, floor 1 - 1/C_R = 0.8000);
        Pr[no answer] = 0.177778;  Pr[an answer that abides] = 0.111111;  violation probability GIVEN that an answer is produced = 0.864865
    u = 4, K = 30, C_R = 100, one pair seen, unseen input: Pr[violating answer] = 0.880000 (formula 0.880000, floor 1 - 1/C_R = 0.9900);
        Pr[no answer] = 0.008889;  Pr[an answer that abides] = 0.111111;  violation probability GIVEN that an answer is produced = 0.887892
(C) Theorem 4.2 and Corollary 4.3 on 1289 random closed classes of semimeasures (phi not injective in general, data with repeats, exact rationals):
    max of [posterior mean of prod 1/(1-v)]/C_R = 1.0000 (<= 1);  max of [posterior mean of sum v]/ln C_R = 0.3706 (<= 1);  min of C_R x posterior weight of never-violators = 1.0000 (>= 1)
    indices with likelihood 0 and v = 1 at a data input (the display of Theorem 4.2 has the term 0 x infinity for them): 136
(D) Theorem 4.7, exact over all data sequences of length <= 5, 260 random instances (stochastic humans, semimeasure hypotheses, designed competitors):
    max of Pr[exists n <= 5: inequality (b) fails]/delta = 0.8327 at (inputs, w(P), delta) = (3, 0.0009765625, 0.5);  max of Pr[exists n <= 5: V(D_n) > (K ln2 + ln(1/delta))/n]/delta = 0.0020   (both must be <= 1)
    designed instance (competitor of weight 1/2 whose likelihood ratio doubles or dies): Pr[(b) fails for some n <= 8] = 0.12500, delta = 0.25000
    designed instance (competitor of weight 1/8 whose likelihood ratio doubles or dies): Pr[(b) fails for some n <= 8] = 0.00781, delta = 0.06250
(E) Proposition 4.8(a),(b): closed forms against enumeration of all subsets (exact rationals): True
    the direct form of 4.8(d) for a fixed data set, 1 - V(D) >= M(D)^(1/k): holds in all cases: True (smallest gap 0.0000)
(F) Proposition 4.10(a),(b): closed forms against enumeration (exact rationals, repeated inputs included): True
(G) Theorem 4.11: M_D(b|x) >= (2u/(2u+1))/(1 + 2^(2-c)(4u)^k) on 200 random instances (exact): True (largest bound/value 1.0000)
(H) What the level 'near 1' of Theorem 4.5 is: memorisers whose answer outside the table is a default d, drawn once from a prior lam over
    {b, g}, where g is an answer that abides by R and that the human never gives (so no default explains any data); exact:
    lam(b) = 1.00: Pr[the mixture answers b at an unseen input] = 0.888889 = lam(b) x (value of Theorem 4.5) = 0.888889;  Pr[the abiding default g] = 0.000000;  Pr[f(x)] = 0.055556
    lam(b) = 0.50: Pr[the mixture answers b at an unseen input] = 0.444444 = lam(b) x (value of Theorem 4.5) = 0.444444;  Pr[the abiding default g] = 0.444444;  Pr[f(x)] = 0.055556
    lam(b) = 0.02: Pr[the mixture answers b at an unseen input] = 0.017778 = lam(b) x (value of Theorem 4.5) = 0.017778;  Pr[the abiding default g] = 0.871111;  Pr[f(x)] = 0.055556
(I) Proposition 4.12, exact (u = 4, coin of weight 1/2, humans = all functions, uniform inputs, n <= 4): Pr[bound fails before the first repeated input] = 0.00000 <= delta = 0.5
(I) Proposition 4.12, exact (u = 4, coin of weight 1/64, humans = all functions, uniform inputs, n <= 4): Pr[bound fails before the first repeated input] = 0.00000 <= delta = 0.9
(I) Proposition 4.12, exact (u = 5, coin of weight 1/4, humans = all functions, uniform inputs, n <= 4): Pr[bound fails before the first repeated input] = 0.00000 <= delta = 0.3
(J) Proposition X3 (soft conditioning with a fall-back answer; proper samplers stay proper: True), 1161 random closed classes, theta in {1/10, 1/2, 1}:
    max of [posterior mean of prod 1/(1-(1-theta)v)]/C = 1.0000 (<= 1);  min of C x posterior weight of the never-violators (all of which answer) = 1.0000 (>= 1)
